the white-rumped vulture hasn't gotten  much respect in Bangladesh it's hunched  slate-gray wings bald head and taste for  carrion have marked it as a symbol of  doom but the Raptors bad image isn't  stopping conservationists from trying to  save it from extinction South Asian  governments and conservationists are  spending millions of dollars to pull  this vulture and two other species back  from the brink the plunge of South  Asia's vulture population began in the  1990s the epidemic stretched across the  subcontinent hitting three vulture  species the white-rumped vulture has  declined by 99% in India the population  went from 10 million to around 6,000 the  other two species have followed similar  trajectories in 2004 scientists and  pakistan found the culprit by examining  the carcasses of cattle some species of  vulture can't metabolize diclofenac a  painkiller that became widely used in  the 1990s to treat cattle when the birds  feed on tainted carcasses uric acid  crystallizes in their kidneys kidney  failure and death soon follow  vultures species that are able to  metabolize the drug haven't suffered  population collapsed the disappearance  of these vultures translates into the  loss of one of nature's tiniest ways to  dispose of a dead body a crew of  vultures can strip a dead cow to its  bones in less than an hour moving a  source of stench and potential disease  governments in the region have responded  by banning the veterinary version of  diclofenac in most places however  veterinarians can still use diclofenac  most common alternatives which are also  toxic to vultures the proliferation of  problem drugs and the reluctance of  governments to ban all of them puts a  premium on finding more benign  medications  a variety of veterinary painkillers are  currently being studied for their  effects on vultures but this process is  time consuming and many drugs remain on  the market that haven't been tested on  vultures meanwhile bird numbers are so  low that even a small crack in the bands  could prove catastrophic a 2004 study  estimated that diclofenac contamination  of as few as one in 760 cow carcasses  could drive down vulture numbers to  prevent further declines  conservationists and collaboration with  governments are trying to create  vultures safe zones in Bangladesh in  2014 the government designated two  vultures zones a circle with a 100  kilometer radius centered on a spot  where imperiled vultures gathered inside  these safe zones conservationists  quartered veterinarians with educational  material warning of the perils of some  drugs and free stockpiles of meloxicam a  painkiller that's safe for vultures some  evidence suggests this strategy is  having an impact surveys found nearly  all pharmacies in Bangladesh stopped  selling diclofenac by the end of 2015  down from 25% in 2014 in November 2017  researchers in Nepal released 17  white-rumped vultures tagged with  satellite tracking systems the first to  be tracked this way if these birds along  with others released in the coming year  survived until April 2020 without a  drug-related fatality the region will be  declared an official vulture safe zone
 

